          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Euripides</strong><br>\nEuripides was born around 480 BCE, in the island of Salamis. His family was of a higher status, allowing him to receive a good education. He was trained in athletics and painting, but his passion for literature and theater soon became apparent. From an early age, Euripides questioned traditional beliefs and the existence of the gods, which later became a theme in his works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Greek Tragedy</strong><br>\nEuripides is known for his innovative contributions to Greek tragedy. He introduced strong female characters and intelligent slaves, and he often focused on the inner lives and motivations of his characters. Unlike his predecessors, who emphasized the role of fate, Euripides explored the psychology of individuals in crisis.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Euripides’ Writing Style</strong><br>\nEuripides differentiated himself from other playwrights through his writing style. He used prologues to explain background stories and deus ex machina, a plot device where a god resolves the human conflicts, at the end of plays. His language was noted for its poetic beauty and his use of rhetorical questions to engage the audience.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
