          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Trojan Women</strong><br>\nOne of Euripides’ most famous plays is \"The Trojan Women,\" which portrayed the suffering of Troy's women after the city's fall. It serves as a commentary on the savagery of war and the plight of the conquered. This play has been praised for its empathetic portrayal of women's suffering and has remained relevant throughout history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Medea and Portrayal of Women</strong><br>\nMedea is another significant work by Euripides. It tells the story of a woman who takes revenge against her husband for his betrayal. The play is unique for its time because it presents a powerful and intelligent woman as the central character. Medea's complex character challenges the traditional views of women in ancient Greek society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use of Chorus</strong><br>\nThe chorus in Euripides' plays often serves a complex role, sometimes participating in the action, while at other times commenting on it. He used the chorus to express doubt, moral ambiguity, and to question societal norms. This innovative use of the chorus allowed Euripides to explore themes of morality and justice more deeply.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
