          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Other Famous Works</strong><br>\nIn addition to \"The Trojan Women\" and \"Medea,\" Euripides wrote many other plays that are still studied today, such as \"Hippolytus\" and \"The Bacchae.\" These plays often depict the conflict between human passion and divine will, a recurrent theme in his body of work. His plays remain celebrated for their structure, characters, and themes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Later Generations</strong><br>\nEuripides' influence extends beyond the boundaries of ancient Greece. His plays have been adapted in various cultures and are considered precursors to modern drama and psychology. His ability to delve into the human psyche has been particularly influential in the development of character-driven narratives.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Euripides</strong><br>\nEuripides died in 406 BCE, but his legacy continues. He is counted among the great tragedians of ancient Greece, alongside Aeschylus and Sophocles. Seventeen of his plays have survived in full, and they continue to be performed and read for their literary and historical value. Euripides is remembered as a playwright who dared to challenge societal norms and explored the complexity of human emotions.</p>\n"
              },
